WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:08.260
A circle, a curved surface and another circle.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:09.860
This is a cylinder!

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:13.280
A right circular cylinder to be precise.

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:16.540
Every cylinder has a central axis,

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:19.780
which passes through the centers of the two circles.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:23.660
The areas of the two circles are equal.

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:26.540
That's because the cylinder we consider

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:28.940
has a uniform cross-section.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:34.200
This tells us that the radii of the two circles will be equal.

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:36.300
In a right circular cylinder,

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:40.940
the distance between the two centers of the circles is its height.

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:42.980
Let's call it 'h'.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:47.060
So how do we find the total surface area of the cylinder?

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:50.540
It will be the sum of areas of the two circles,

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:53.760
and the area of the curved surface.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:00.020
As the radius is 'r' the area of each circle will be 'pi r squared'.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:03.200
So the sum of the areas of both the circles,

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:06.680
will be 'pi r' squared plus 'pi r' squared.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:10.240
But what is the area of the curved surface?

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:12.320
This is interesting!

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:14.820
The curved surface when opened up,

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:17.400
will look like a rectangle.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:21.060
And what will be the length and the breadth of this rectangle?

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:25.220
The breadth is easy! It will be the height of the cylinder.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:27.640
What about the length?

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:30.120
Yes, the length of the rectangle,

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:33.040
will be the circumference of the circle.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.600
It will be '2 pi r'.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:40.680
It was easy! All we did was open this curved surface up.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:44.860
Anyway, these two together add up to '2 pi r squared'.

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:49.800
And the area of this rectangle will be '2 pi rh'.

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:54.260
That's it. This is the total surface area of a cylinder

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:58.000
with radius of the base 'r' and height 'h'.

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:00.860
As '2 pi r' is common to both the terms.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:05.460
We can write it as '2 pi r' times 'r plus h'.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:08.360
That's the total surface area.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:10.620
What is this blue part then?

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:14.320
It's called the curved surface area of the cylinder.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:16.460
'2 Pi rh'.

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:20.440
And finally we come to the volume of a cylinder.

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:24.900
It equals the area of its base times its height.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:29.480
As the base is circular, its area will be 'pi r squared'.

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:31.460
And its height is 'h'.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.020
That's the volume of a cylinder.

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:36.500
pi r squared h.

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:39.300
You don't have to memorize any of this.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:42.700
You can easily derive the total surface area,

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:44.300
and for the volume,

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:47.600
you just need to know that it is the area of its base

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:50.820
or its cross section times its height.

